  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 PM MDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows broad but shallow upper trough across \nWestern Canada this afternoon, with pair of shortwaves across \nthe PacNW. Moisture spreading from Oregon into Idaho today, \nincreasing clouds across the region. Upstream radar shows weak \nshowers across northern Nevada, and today's High-res CAMs bring \nthreat of weak showers across the region this evening and \novernight. Surface humidities are very dry, so believe threat \nfor actual precipitation is low until lower overnight \ntemperatures and deeper moisture can improve the potential. \nInstability is unimpressive, so no thunderstorm threat expected.\nEven at that, precipitation should mainly be limited to \nportions of the eastern highlands, and amounts will remain \nlight. Winds today are struggling to develop, but HRRR still \npinpointing a later afternoon into evening peak for speeds. \nSimilar wind conditions are expected for tomorrow, absent the \nprecipitation, along with slightly cooler temperatures. \n\nDeeper shortwave associated with the broad upper low shifts into\nIdaho Saturday, bringing an increased chance of precipitation\nSaturday night into Sunday, mainly for northern zones across the\ncentral mountains and Divide. Temperatures cool significantly\ninto Sunday with highs generally expected to range in the upper\n60s to lower 70s. Headed into next work week, there are timing\nand amplitude differences between operational GFS and ECMWF runs\nthis morning. Ensembles and ensemble clusters maintain a broad\ntrough across the western US, trending toward a split flow\npattern towards the latter half of the week, leading to a fair\namount of uncertainty toward temperature and precipitation\ntrends throughout the week.",
